#pragma once

#include "/Plugin/CloudAtmosphere/Private/GasGiantConstants.ush"


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gas giant banding and flow. One baked volume, one simulated flow field.
//
// WHY GAS GIANTS BAND, since the whole design follows from it.
//
// Rapid rotation makes the flow quasi-2D. Two-dimensional turbulence cascades
// energy UPWARD in scale rather than downward, so structure grows instead of
// dissipating. On a sphere the Coriolis parameter varies with latitude, which
// makes that cascade anisotropic: it feeds east-west modes preferentially,
// because north-south motion radiates Rossby waves and east-west motion does
// not. The cascade arrests at the Rhines scale, into zonal jets.
//
// This file used to CONSTRUCT that: prescribed differential rotation applied to
// baked isotropic noise. That was a defensible model -- banding really is
// anisotropic straining of isotropic structure -- but a kinematic shear has no
// way to SUSTAIN anything, so every vortex was eventually drawn into a filament
// and the field had to be periodically reset behind a crossfade.
//
// The flow is now simulated. What this file does is turn a flow field into
// cloud, which is what it was always for.
//
// THE THREE OPERATIONS, deliberately separated.
//
//   Structure   Filaments and whorls. Comes from the simulated field, which
//               carries eddies that persist because they are fed.
//
//   Transport   Bands and their contents moving. The sim's velocity carries
//               the jets; the planet's own rotation is one GG_RotateZ.
//
//   Evolution   Things changing rather than merely moving. The sim again --
//               it is temporally coherent, so nothing here accumulates.
//
// WHY ROTATION AND NOT DISPLACEMENT, for the rigid spin.
//
// No offset added to a coordinate produces orbiting; it produces scrolling
// across an axis, and on a sphere it also drags latitude. Displacing along the
// east vector by c and renormalizing moves along a CHORD, so the length becomes
// sqrt(1 + c*c) and the latitude creeps equatorward as c grows.
//
// Rotation about Z preserves P.z and |P| identically. Every latitude ring maps
// onto itself, the Jacobian determinant is exactly 1, and there is no
// accumulation error to reset. A latitude-only pattern is invariant under it,
// which is the precise statement that zonal flow cannot disturb its own bands
// -- so all band meander comes from the warp, and the warp now carries real
// eddies rather than constructed ones.
//
// THE INPUTS.
//
//   FlowField    Texture2DArray from the sim. RGB is tangent velocity as an
//                ANGULAR RATE in planet-local space; A is the streamfunction,
//                read as pressure. Velocity is N x grad(psi), so circulation
//                and pressure come from one field and vortex centres in alpha
//                are exactly what the velocity circles. Sampled by direction.
//                One slice per simulated layer, so a deeper slice can advect
//                the detail deck and produce real vertical wind shear.
//
//                SAMPLER: WRAP ON U, CLAMP ON V. The grid is a cylinder.
//
//   PackedDetail Four independent scalars, BGRA8. The ONLY baked volume left.
//
//                  R  Perlin        )  combined as Perlin-Worley, the fluffy
//                  G  Worley F1     )  cloud-shape term. G also gates towers.
//                  B  Ridged        streaks, read raw so they stay wispy
//                  A  Worley F2-F1  the filament network
//
//                Sampled TWICE, at two positions: once carrying the flow warp
//                so filaments stretch along it, once held nearly rigid so the
//                chemistry stays round. Same texture, two behaviours.
//
//                The second volume is gone. It carried a baked gradient for
//                the detail warp, and the warp now comes from the sim.
//
// THE VOLUME IS WRAP ON ALL THREE AXES.
//
/** Steps in the warp integration.
 *
 *  Each step takes 1/N of the displacement and re-samples where it arrived, so
 *  the path follows the field rather than leaving along a single tangent. What
 *  that buys is the streamline's CURVATURE -- one step moves material once and
 *  stops, so the shape of the streamline never appears in the output no matter
 *  what the field holds.
 *
 *  Costs one fetch per step. Cheaper than it was: there is one phase now rather
 *  than two, so 4 here costs what 2 used to. */
#ifndef GG_WARP_ITERATIONS
#define GG_WARP_ITERATIONS 4
#endif

/** Replace the returned cloud top with the step count, normalized against
 *  InMaxSteps, for a cost heatmap. */
#ifndef GG_DEBUG_STEPS
#define GG_DEBUG_STEPS 0
#endif

/** Enable the second warp octave and its ridge channel. Two extra fetches: the
 *  baked detail volume, and the deeper simulated layer that advects it. The
 *  first thing to switch off if the march gets expensive. */
#ifndef GG_DETAIL_OCTAVE
#define GG_DETAIL_OCTAVE 1
#endif

/** Include the packed detail layer. One extra fetch. */
#ifndef GG_PACKED_DETAIL
#define GG_PACKED_DETAIL 1
#endif

/** Rotation about the planet's axis. The only transport primitive used here.
 *
 *  Exact for any angle, so it never needs to be small and never needs a reset.
 *  Preserves P.z, which is what leaves the bands undisturbed. */
float3 GG_RotateZ(float3 P, float Angle)
{
    float SinA, CosA;
    sincos(Angle, SinA, CosA);

    return float3(
		P.x * CosA - P.y * SinA,
		P.x * SinA + P.y * CosA,
		P.z);
}

/** Perlin dilated by inverted Worley -- the standard cloud-shape combine.
 *
 *  Perlin alone is smooth undulation with no sense of a cloud EDGE. Worley F1
 *  inverted is billowy blobs with no interior variation. Remapping the first by
 *  the second keeps Perlin's detail while giving it Worley's rounded boundary,
 *  and that rounded boundary is what reads as fluffiness.
 *
 *  This has to happen in the shader rather than the bake because the baker's
 *  channels are independent by construction -- there is no cross-channel
 *  operation in the recipe model, and adding one would compromise the thing
 *  that makes the probe, grouping and brick bounds work. Two channels out, one
 *  combine in, no fetch cost.
 *
 *  Worley here is the raw F1, which is LOW at cell centres, so the remap floor
 *  uses it directly rather than inverted. */
float GG_PerlinWorley(float Perlin, float WorleyF1)
{
    const float Low = -WorleyF1;

    return saturate((Perlin - Low) / max(1.0f - Low, 1e-3f));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The field
//
// WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY MOST OF THIS FILE GOT SHORTER.
//
// The flow used to be constructed here: a prescribed differential rotation
// applied to baked isotropic noise, integrated over a warp, with the whole
// crossfade apparatus wrapped around it to bound winding that would otherwise
// run away. That model was defensible -- banding really is anisotropic
// straining of isotropic structure -- but it had no mechanism to SUSTAIN
// anything. Shear only ever stretches, so every vortex was eventually drawn
// into a filament, which is why spots had to be exempted from the shear by
// hand and why the field needed periodic resetting at all.
//
// The flow now comes from a simulation. Velocity arrives as a texture, already
// carrying jets, eddies and vortices that persist because they are fed rather
// than because they were excluded. So the constructing goes away and what is
// left here is what this file was always for: turning a flow field into cloud.
//
// DELETED, all of it existing only to manage unbounded kinematic shear:
// the crossfade, SpinUpTime, CrossfadePeriod, PhaseJitterAmount, ShearFalloff,
// Swirl, the two-phase evaluation, and the cat's-eye construction that made
// closed streamlines by choosing a co-moving frame. The sim has closed
// streamlines because the fluid does.
//
// WHAT THE WARP IS FOR NOW, WHICH IS NOT WHAT IT WAS FOR.
//
// It used to integrate the whole history of the flow, and its length was how
// wound the planet was. It is now a SHORT advection -- a fraction of an eddy
// turnover -- whose only job is to carry the fine detail volumes along the
// current flow. The history lives in the sim, which is temporally coherent and
// regenerates structure continuously, so nothing here has to accumulate.
//
// That is what makes it bounded by construction rather than by a crossfade,
// and it is the whole reason the sim was worth building.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Samples the simulated flow field by DIRECTION.
 *
 *  The sim grid is longitude by sin(latitude), which makes both coordinates
 *  cheap from a unit vector:
 *
 *      U = frac(atan2(N.y, N.x) / 2pi)      one transcendental
 *      V = 0.5 + 0.5 * N.z                  free, because the grid is in sin
 *
 *  frac() rather than a branch on the sign: atan2 returns (-pi, pi], and frac
 *  of a negative value already wraps to the positive end, so the seam is
 *  handled by the same instruction that does the scaling.
 *
 *  SAMPLER STATE MUST BE WRAP ON U AND CLAMP ON V. The grid is a cylinder, not
 *  a torus: longitude wraps, latitude does not, and a wrapping V joins the
 *  north pole to the south.
 *
 *  Returns float4(u_east, u_north, psi_eddy, band):
 *
 *    xy  Tangent velocity components, in radians per unit time. Rebuild the 3D
 *        vector with GG_FlowVelocity. Stored as two components because a
 *        tangent vector has two degrees of freedom, and the third channel is
 *        worth more elsewhere.
 *
 *    z   The EDDY streamfunction: psi with its zonal mean removed. Zero in
 *        undisturbed flow, positive in anticyclones, negative in cyclones.
 *        Raw psi would be dominated by its latitude ramp, so every use of it
 *        would land on bands rather than on storms.
 *
 *    w   Normalized vorticity, near [-1,1]. THIS IS THE BAND SIGNAL. Bands are
 *        what vorticity looks like, and because the sim transports vorticity,
 *        this arrives already meandering and already wrapped around vortices --
 *        no warping in the material is needed to produce that.
 *
 *  Units are physical throughout and normalization is done sim-side, so there
 *  is no calibration constant here and nothing to keep in sync by hand. */
float4 GG_SampleFlow(Texture2DArray FlowField, SamplerState FlowSampler, float3 N, float Layer)
{
    const float U = frac(atan2(N.y, N.x) * (0.5f / PI));
    const float V = 0.5f + 0.5f * N.z;

    return FlowField.SampleLevel(FlowSampler, float3(U, V, Layer), 0.0f);
}

/** Rebuilds the 3D tangent velocity from the stored components.
 *
 *  NO TRIG. With c = length(N.xy) the tangent basis is
 *
 *      east  = (-N.y, N.x, 0) / c
 *      north = (-N.z*N.x/c, -N.z*N.y/c, c)
 *
 *  which are the same vectors sincos(longitude) would give, obtained from N
 *  directly. That is what makes storing two components cheaper than storing
 *  three: about ten ALU against a whole texture channel.
 *
 *  Guarded at the poles, where c goes to zero and east is undefined. The sim
 *  grid is cell-centred so no sampled texel sits exactly on a pole, but a
 *  shading point can. */
float3 GG_FlowVelocity(float3 N, float4 Flow)
{
    const float C = max(length(N.xy), 1e-5f);

    const float3 East = float3(-N.y, N.x, 0.0f) / C;
    const float3 North = float3(-N.z * N.x / C, -N.z * N.y / C, C);

    return East * Flow.x + North * Flow.y;
}

/** Density as a function of depth below the cloud top.
 *
 *  ONE CURVE, STARTING AT EXACTLY ZERO. That is the whole point of it.
 *
 *  This used to be two mechanisms: a smoothstep envelope climbing from 0 to 1
 *  across a softness band, and then a linear gain ramping upward from a base of
 *  1. The handover sat at an arbitrary altitude and started from an arbitrary
 *  density, and both of those are visible -- density that arrives at the deck
 *  top already finite draws a hard line there, and a deck whose fade completes
 *  before the ramp begins has a shoulder in it.
 *
 *      rho(d) = Core * (1 - exp(-(d * Ramp)^(2/Curve)))
 *
 *  Ramp is an inverse depth: it moves the whole curve inward, so a larger value
 *  reaches core density in less depth.
 *
 *  CURVE IS THE ONSET SHARPNESS, AND THE EXPONENT IS INVERTED FOR IT.
 *
 *  The natural reading of an exponent is backwards here. Raising the power on
 *  (d*Ramp) makes it SMALLER near the top, because d*Ramp is below one there --
 *  so a bigger exponent delays the onset and the deck goes more transparent at
 *  its surface, which is the opposite of what a sharpness control should do.
 *
 *  Written as 2/Curve instead, so the parameter reads the way it should:
 *
 *      Curve 1    exponent 2, the default. Zero derivative at the top, so the
 *                 onset is genuinely smooth and there is no crease.
 *      Curve 2    exponent 1, a plain exponential. Finite slope meeting the
 *                 zero outside, which is a C1 kink along the whole cloud top.
 *      Curve 4    exponent 1/2. Infinite slope: a hard edge at the deck.
 *      Curve 0.5  exponent 4. Very soft, the deck fades in over a long depth.
 *
 *  So higher is faster and sharper, lower is softer, and the crease that this
 *  whole arrangement exists to avoid arrives at 2 and gets worse above it. That
 *  is a real trade rather than a bug: a sharp cloud top IS a discontinuity, and
 *  the parameter is where to make that choice deliberately. */
float GG_DensityRamp(float Depth, float Ramp, float Curve, float Core)
{
    const float D = max(Depth, 0.0f) * max(Ramp, 0.0f);

	// Inverted, so larger Curve means a smaller exponent and a faster onset.
	// Clamped below because a non-positive exponent makes pow(0, k) return one,
	// which would put full core density above the deck.
    const float Exponent = 2.0f / max(Curve, 0.05f);

    return Core * (1.0f - exp(-pow(D, Exponent)));
}


/** Cloud top altitude, as a fraction of the shell thickness.
 *
 *  Flow first: band elevation, pressure lift, storm towers. Then the detail
 *  carve, in the same form the density function uses.
 *
 *  DetailRelief is separate from BandRelief on purpose. The two were once
 *  summed before the relief multiply, so raising BandRelief for thicker bands
 *  multiplied the fine noise by the same factor -- and a noise field stretched
 *  vertically but not horizontally becomes spikes.
 *
 *  Relief is float4(TopBase, BandRelief, PressureLift, StormTowers).
 *
 *  ReliefScale collapses the band relief toward flat where the flow is
 *  straining hard, and deliberately does NOT touch the pressure lift or the
 *  storm towers: those are vertical motion, not stretched material, so thinning
 *  them would flatten storms precisely where the flow is most active.
 *
 *  The pressure sign is not arbitrary. Anticyclones are high pressure, rising,
 *  and condense fresh ammonia above the haze -- they are the bright ovals and
 *  the Great Red Spot, and they must sit HIGH. Cyclones sink and expose deeper
 *  material, so they must sit low. Getting this backwards produces a field that
 *  reads as plausible until you notice every storm is a pit. */
float GG_CloudTop(
	float Elevation,
	float Shape,
	float DetailRelief,
	float Pressure,
	float StormCells,
	float ReliefScale,
	float4 Relief,
	float VortexThreshold)
{
	// Elevation runs 0 at a band boundary to 1 in a band interior, so Relief.y
	// is the whole height between a jet and a zone. It wants to be large: the
	// point of driving height from the flow is that the bands are geometry
	// rather than a pattern painted on a sphere.
    float Top = Relief.x + ReliefScale * Relief.y * (Elevation - 0.5f);

	// DETAIL AS SURFACE RELIEF, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT EFFECT FROM THE CARVE.
	//
	// Displacing the deck gives a lumpy SURFACE with a sharp top; eroding the
	// density gives a soft, penetrable skin. They are not two ways of doing one
	// thing and both are worth having, so both are here with separate amounts.
	//
	// ONE CAVEAT WORTH KNOWING. With DetailVertical above zero the shape varies
	// with altitude, so this "top" is not a single surface -- it is a threshold
	// that shifts slightly along the ray. That is a blur rather than an error:
	// the ramp starts at a different depth for each sample, which softens the
	// top instead of defining it sharply. At DetailVertical zero the sample is
	// columnar and the deck is a true heightfield with a crisp silhouette.
	//
	// Which of those is wanted is a look decision, and DetailVertical is where
	// it gets made.
	//
	// EXACTLY THE FORM THE DENSITY CARVE USES, expressed as altitude.
	//
	// The density carve is lerp(1, Shape, amount): it pulls density DOWN where
	// Shape is low and leaves it alone where Shape is one. This is the same
	// statement about height -- the deck drops by DetailRelief where Shape is
	// zero and is untouched where Shape is one.
	//
	// Same Shape, so it is already edge-weighted and already the mix of all
	// three detail channels. One noise, two consequences: the deck's SHAPE and
	// the skin's DENSITY.
	//
	// Subtractive rather than centred. A centred displacement raises as much as
	// it lowers, which just re-tunes Relief.x on average and makes the two
	// applications disagree about which way the noise cuts. Carving down only
	// also keeps the bound tight: TopMax is untouched, so the grazing chord
	// does not grow.
	//
	// Not scaled by ReliefScale, matching the density carve, which is not
	// either. Strain thins the BAND relief; it has no claim on the detail.
    Top -= DetailRelief * (1.0f - Shape);

    Top += Relief.z * Pressure;

	// Convective towers, only where the circulation supports one. Ungated,
	// Worley F1 is polka dots; gated on vortex strength it becomes storms that
	// sit where the flow says they should. Inverted because F1 is minimal at
	// cell CENTRES, which is where the tower belongs.
    const float Vortex = smoothstep(VortexThreshold, 1.0f, abs(Pressure));

    Top += Relief.w * (1.0f - StormCells) * Vortex;

    return Top;
}


/** Evaluates the cloud field at a direction on the sphere.
 *
 *  Returns:
 *    x  Cloud top altitude, as a fraction of the shell thickness.
 *    y  Pressure. Negative is a low, so cyclonic; extrema are vortex centres
 *       and the circulation already matches them.
 *    z  BAND, signed and unclamped. Negative is one band type, positive the
 *       other, zero the boundary between them. Feed to GG_BandColour.
 *    w  SHAPE in [0,1], sampled in true 3D at Height. 1 is solid, 0 is clear.
 *       The density function multiplies by it; it never moves the deck.
 *
 *  Scales  float4(DetailScale, PackedScale, DetailWarpInherit, PackedWarpInherit)
 *  Warps   float4(WarpTime, DetailWarp, BandJitter, TurbulenceFloor)
 *  Detail  float4(Ridge, Mottle, Wisp, EdgeBias)
 *  Layers  float3(FlowLayer, DeepFlowLayer, DeckSlope)
 *
 *  Three fetches per evaluation, against eight before. */
float4 GG_Field(
	Texture2DArray FlowField,
	SamplerState FlowSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 P,
	float Time,
	float RigidRate,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float3 Layers,
	float Height,
	float BandSharpness,
	float ReliefThinning,
	float DetailVertical,
	float DetailRelief,
	float4 Relief,
	float VortexThreshold)
{
	// The planet's own rotation, and the only rigid term left. The sim runs in
	// the rotating frame, so its velocities are relative to this; the warp
	// window is far too short to accumulate a rotation, and a latitude-only
	// pattern is invariant under one anyway, so this has to be applied to the
	// SAMPLING POSITION rather than left to the flow.
    const float3 PSpun = GG_RotateZ(P, -RigidRate * Time);
    const float3 N = normalize(PSpun);
    const float S = N.z;

	// -- The band, straight from the flow ------------------------------------
	//
	// One sample, before the warp, used for three things: the band itself, the
	// pressure, and the turbulence weight that scales the warp. Sampling here
	// rather than inside the loop costs nothing -- the loop's first iteration
	// sampled this same position anyway -- and it breaks what would otherwise
	// be circular, since the warp length depends on the band.
	//
	// UNWARPED, deliberately. The sim transports vorticity, so the band pattern
	// arrives already advected: meandering, wrapped around vortices, carrying
	// the whole history of the flow. Warping the lookup on top of that would be
	// double-counting a transport that has already happened.
    const float4 Flow0 = GG_SampleFlow(FlowField, FlowSampler, N, Layers.x);

    const float Pressure = Flow0.z;

	// SIGN AND MAGNITUDE ARE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS AND DRIVE TWO DIFFERENT
	// OUTPUTS.
	//
	// The debug view renders vorticity as a diverging ramp: which colour is the
	// SIGN, and how bright is the MAGNITUDE. Those read as one image but they
	// are not one quantity, and collapsing them into a single bipolar channel
	// is what made the material's output stop resembling the debug view.
	//
	// Contrast applied once, here, before the split, so both halves stay
	// consistent with each other. Warps.z biases the balance first, which
	// shifts which band type dominates without retuning the sim.
    const float Zeta = (Flow0.w + Warps.z) * BandSharpness;

	// WHICH BAND, and by how much. SIGNED AND UNCLAMPED.
	//
	// Carried out raw rather than remapped into [0,1], because the colour ramp
	// wants both halves of it and a saturate here would throw away the range
	// before the ramp ever sees it -- a scale applied downstream would then be
	// re-expanding a value that had already lost its ends.
	//
	// This is exactly the quantity the debug view feeds its diverging ramp, so
	// GG_BandColour with a black edge colour reproduces that view precisely.
    const float BandSigned = Zeta;

	// HOW STRONG: the elevation. Zero at a band boundary, one at a band core --
	// so boundaries sit LOW and band interiors pile UP, which is the brightness
	// of the debug view read as height.
	//
	// That is also the right way round physically. Band boundaries are the
	// jets, where the shear is; band interiors are quiescent. Material gets
	// thinned where it is strained and accumulates where it is not.
	// SMOOTH, NOT saturate(abs()). That form has a kink where it pins at one
	// and another at the zero crossing, and both are C1 discontinuities in the
	// cloud top -- creases running along band contours, which read as hard
	// lines in the surface rather than as banding.
	//
	// 1 - exp(-Zeta^2) is C-infinity everywhere: quadratic near zero, so band
	// boundaries are rounded valleys rather than V-shaped ones, and asymptotic
	// to one, so band cores flatten without ever pinning.
    const float Elevation = 1.0f - exp(-Zeta * Zeta);

	// The boundary weight falls straight out of the elevation: a boundary is
	// exactly where the elevation is lowest. One subtract, and it cannot drift
	// out of step with the band the way a separately-derived weight could.
    const float Boundary = 1.0f - Elevation;

	// Turbulence follows the shear, and the shear lives at the boundaries. Zone
	// interiors go nearly laminar, which stops the bands reading as one
	// scalloped curve repeated all the way round. Warps.w is the floor, since a
	// real zone interior is calmer than its edges but not glass.
    const float Turbulence = lerp(Warps.w, 1.0f, Boundary);

    const float StepTime = (Warps.x * Turbulence) / (float) GG_WARP_ITERATIONS;

    float3 Pos = N;

	// Kept so the strain can be measured from the flow itself rather than from
	// an analytic profile. See the relief thinning note below.
    float3 FirstVelocity = 0.0f;
    float3 LastVelocity = 0.0f;

	[unroll]
    for (int It = 0; It < GG_WARP_ITERATIONS; It++)
    {
		// Reuses the pre-loop sample on the first iteration, so the extra
		// fetch above is genuinely free.
        const float4 Flow = (It == 0) ? Flow0
			: GG_SampleFlow(FlowField, FlowSampler, Pos, Layers.x);

        const float3 Velocity = GG_FlowVelocity(Pos, Flow);

        if (It == 0)
        {
            FirstVelocity = Velocity;
        }

        LastVelocity = Velocity;

		// Already tangent, already an angular rate, already containing both the
		// jets and the eddies. The old form needed a tangential projection, a
		// cross product, a separate analytic zonal term and a unit conversion;
		// all four are now properties of the field rather than steps here.
		//
		// Renormalized every step because the displacement leaves the sphere.
		// Only .z is read downstream, so a chord move would bias latitude, and
		// over several steps it compounds.
        Pos = normalize(Pos + Velocity * StepTime);
    }

    const float3 Warped = Pos;

	// Net displacement as a difference rather than a running sum: each step
	// renormalizes, so the sum of the steps is not the distance travelled.
    const float3 FlowWarp = Warped - N;

    float Ridge = 0.5f;
    float3 DetailWarp = 0.0f;

#if GG_DETAIL_OCTAVE

	// -- Detail: baked, warped by a DEEPER simulated layer -------------------
	//
	// Layers.y selects which slice of the stack drives this. Reading a
	// different layer from the coarse warp is what produces genuine vertical
	// wind shear: the two decks are advected by flows that disagree, because
	// the sim maintains them under different jet profiles.
	//
	// Inherits only a FRACTION of the coarse displacement. A displacement field
	// with a large gradient IS strain, whatever it is called, so inheriting one
	// whole imposes an order-one strain on the fine layer regardless of its own
	// flow. Physically the fraction is right too: a small eddy is regenerated
	// before it accumulates much strain, so it should be advected by a recent
	// slice of the flow's history rather than all of it.

    float3 PDetail = normalize(N + FlowWarp * Scales.z);

    const float4 DeepFlow = GG_SampleFlow(FlowField, FlowSampler, PDetail, Layers.y);

	// SAMPLED IN TRUE 3D, at the shading point's own altitude.
	//
	// PDetail is a unit direction, so PDetail * Scale traces a sphere through
	// the volume -- a 2D slice, throwing away every bit of vertical structure
	// the bake put there. Adding the altitude term walks radially through the
	// noise as the march climbs, so the volume is read as the volume it is.
	//
	// DetailVertical is how many noise units the shell thickness spans. It sets
	// the vertical feature size against Scales.x's horizontal one, so their
	// ratio is the aspect of the resulting structure -- which is the honest
	// version of the aspect-ratio control that a radial DISPLACEMENT of a 2D
	// slice was only ever faking.
	//
	// NO INDEPENDENT DRIFT. The volume moves because the flow moves it. It used
	// to scroll through its own domain on a clock, which made the detail change
	// whether or not anything was happening to it -- grain by definition. If it
	// looks pinned while the bands move over it, raise Warps.x rather than
	// reintroducing a clock.
    const float3 DetailUVW = PDetail * (Scales.x + Height * DetailVertical);
    const float4 DetailSample = PackedDetail.SampleLevel(PackedSampler, DetailUVW, 0.0f);

    DetailWarp = GG_FlowVelocity(PDetail, DeepFlow) * (Warps.y * Turbulence);
    Pos = normalize(Warped + DetailWarp);

	// Alpha carries Worley F2-F1, the filament network. Minimal ON cell walls,
	// so it arrives dark and reads as bright wisps once weighted.
	//
	// SAME VOLUME AS THE PACKED SAMPLE BELOW, read at a DIFFERENT POSITION.
	//
	// The second baked volume is gone. It used to carry a gradient field for
	// this warp, and the warp now comes from the sim -- so all that survived of
	// it was one alpha channel in an RGBA16F volume, which is four times the
	// storage for a quarter of the use.
	//
	// The two positions are still worth keeping apart, and that is why this is
	// a separate fetch rather than a swizzle of the one below. PDetail inherits
	// the flow warp at Scales.z, so filaments stretch along the flow; PPacked
	// inherits almost none of it, so the chemistry stays round. Same texture,
	// two behaviours.
    Ridge = DetailSample.a;
#endif

    const float3 FinalWarped = Pos;

    float4 Packed = 0.5f;

#if GG_PACKED_DETAIL

	// -- Packed: chemistry riding on top, held nearly rigid -------------------
	//
	// Its own inheritance, separate from the detail layer's, and 0 is a
	// legitimate setting rather than a degenerate one. This layer's job is to
	// break the flow up into rounded organic shapes, and a shape dragged
	// through the flow field is no longer round. The other layers want to
	// follow the flow because they ARE the flow; this one reads better held
	// rigid than stretched along with everything else.

    const float3 PPacked = normalize(N + (FlowWarp + DetailWarp) * Scales.w);

    const float3 PackedUVW = PPacked * (Scales.y + Height * DetailVertical);
    Packed = PackedDetail.SampleLevel(PackedSampler, PackedUVW, 0.0f);
#endif


	// RELIEF THINNING, MEASURED FROM THE FLOW RATHER THAN PRESCRIBED.
	//
	// Shear stretches a parcel horizontally without adding material to it, so
	// an incompressible parcel stretched by g in one direction and unchanged in
	// the other must thin VERTICALLY by 1/g. Heavily strained regions belong
	// lower and flatter, and this is what the fluid would actually do rather
	// than a correction applied to make it behave.
	//
	// Without it, filament width shrinks with strain while filament height does
	// not, so the aspect ratio runs away and the march reads them as spikes --
	// fine at distance, sharp acute peaks up close.
	//
	// The strain now comes from the WARP PATH, not from the jet profile. The
	// velocity difference between the start and end of the path, divided by the
	// distance travelled, is a directional derivative of velocity along the
	// streamline -- which is a strain rate, in the right units, for free: both
	// samples were already taken by the loop above.
	//
	// That is a real improvement over the analytic form, not just a saving. The
	// old version derived strain from the prescribed zonal profile, so it knew
	// nothing about eddies and reported zero thinning inside a vortex that was
	// straining material hard. It also needed JetStrength and EquatorialBoost
	// threaded down here purely to compute it, which meant two parameters that
	// had to be kept in sync with the sim by hand and did nothing else.
	//
	// Multiplied by the warp time to make it dimensionless, and normalized by
	// the band frequency so the parameter stays O(1).
    const float Travelled = length(FlowWarp);

    const float StrainRate = length(LastVelocity - FirstVelocity) / max(Travelled, 1e-5f);

	// BandCount is gone from this file, so the normalization it used to carry
	// folds into ReliefThinning. That is the only thing it affected.
    const float Winding = StrainRate * Warps.x;

    const float ReliefScale = 1.0f / (1.0f + ReliefThinning * Winding);

	// EDGE WEIGHTING. Boundary peaks mid-transition and falls to zero in a
	// saturated zone or belt. Detail applied uniformly is by definition grain:
	// it has no relationship to the structure it sits on. Detail concentrated
	// on the transitions ravels the band EDGES, which is where a real gas
	// giant's billows live.
	//
	// EdgeBias is how much survives in the flat interiors -- zero everywhere
	// but the edges is its own kind of artificial.
    const float Edge = lerp(Detail.w, 1.0f, Boundary);

	// R against G as Perlin-Worley: the fluffy cloud-shape term. B stays raw as
	// streaks, which want to look wispy rather than rounded.
    const float Fluff = GG_PerlinWorley(Packed.r, Packed.g);

	// A WEIGHTED MIX IN [0,1], NOT A SIGNED MODULATION.
	//
	// Every channel here is already unipolar, and the density function
	// MULTIPLIES by this rather than adding it -- so it has to stay a
	// coverage-like quantity, where 1 is solid and 0 is clear.
	//
	// Normalized by the weight sum, so changing the balance between ridge,
	// fluff and wisp does not also change how much cloud there is. Without it
	// the three sliders all double as a density control, which makes them
	// impossible to tune against each other.
    const float WeightSum = max(Detail.x + Detail.y + Detail.z, 1e-4f);

    const float Mix = saturate(
		(Detail.x * Ridge + Detail.y * Fluff + Detail.z * Packed.b) / WeightSum);

	// 1 in band interiors, falling toward the mix at the transitions. So the
	// detail breaks up the edges and leaves the zone interiors coherent.
	//
	// THE SAME SHAPE DRIVES BOTH THE DECK AND THE DENSITY, because they are
	// different effects rather than two ways of doing one thing. Displacing the
	// deck moves where the density ramp STARTS, which changes the silhouette
	// and the shape of the top. Carving the density changes HOW MUCH is there
	// once the ramp has begun, which softens the skin without moving the
	// surface. Running one noise through both is what gives lumps that are also
	// wispy, rather than lumps with a smooth skin or a smooth deck with
	// wispiness painted on it.
    const float Shape = lerp(1.0f, Mix, Edge);



	// Top is assembled HERE rather than in the density function, because the
	// storm cell mask is Packed.g and Packed only exists in this scope.
    const float Top = GG_CloudTop(
		Elevation, Shape, DetailRelief, Pressure, Packed.g,
		ReliefScale, Relief, VortexThreshold);

    return float4(Top, Pressure, BandSigned, Shape);
}

/** Surface normal from the marched cloud-top altitude, using screen
 *  derivatives.
 *
 *  THE NAIVE FORM IS WRONG ON A SPHERE. Treating ddx and ddy of the height as a
 *  tangent-space gradient assumes the screen basis is orthonormal and aligned
 *  with the surface, and it is neither: it is whatever the projection and the
 *  local curvature make it, so the resulting normal tilts with the camera and
 *  stretches toward the limb.
 *
 *  The correct construction inverts the screen-to-surface Jacobian. With dpx
 *  and dpy the world position derivatives and N the sphere normal,
 *
 *      r1 = cross(dpy, N)      r2 = cross(N, dpx)      det = dot(dpx, r1)
 *
 *  gives a basis in which (r1*dhx + r2*dhy)/det is the true surface gradient of
 *  the height, whatever shape the screen basis happens to be. Subtracting it
 *  from the sphere normal tilts the surface by the slope.
 *
 *  HeightScale converts the height into the same units as the world position.
 *  The march returns altitude in SHELL FRACTIONS, so this is the shell
 *  thickness expressed in world units -- Profile.x times whatever scale the
 *  actor carries. Getting it wrong scales the bumpiness, not its direction, so
 *  it is safe to tune by eye.
 *
 *  TWO LIMITS WORTH KNOWING, because both look like bugs.
 *
 *  Screen derivatives are computed per 2x2 quad, so the normal is constant
 *  across each quad and shows as blockiness under magnification. Nothing can be
 *  done about that short of differencing the field analytically.
 *
 *  And the height is a density-weighted MEAN, not a surface. Where a ray grazes
 *  the deck it averages over a long path that has no single altitude, so the
 *  normal there is meaningless -- and at the mesh silhouette the derivative
 *  reaches across to the background and blows up. Weight the result by opacity
 *  and by facing, or the limb will read as a bright rim. */
float3 GG_NormalFromHeight(float3 WorldPos, float3 SphereNormal, float Height, float HeightScale)
{
    const float3 N = normalize(SphereNormal);

    const float3 dpx = ddx(WorldPos);
    const float3 dpy = ddy(WorldPos);

    const float dhx = ddx(Height) * HeightScale;
    const float dhy = ddy(Height) * HeightScale;

    const float3 r1 = cross(dpy, N);
    const float3 r2 = cross(N, dpx);

    const float Det = dot(dpx, r1);

	// Degenerate where the quad projects to nothing, which happens at the
	// silhouette and on backfacing fragments. Returning the sphere normal there
	// is the right failure: flat rather than exploded.
    if (abs(Det) < 1e-12f)
    {
        return N;
    }

    const float3 Gradient = (r1 * dhx + r2 * dhy) / Det;

    return normalize(N - Gradient);
}

/** The band colour ramp. A generalisation of the debug view's diverging ramp.
 *
 *  The debug view picks a colour by the SIGN of vorticity and scales it by the
 *  MAGNITUDE, so zero comes out black and the band cores come out saturated.
 *  That reads considerably better than a straight two-colour lerp, because the
 *  dark line between bands is doing real work: it separates the two families
 *  instead of blending them through a muddy midpoint.
 *
 *  Generalised only in that the dark end is a parameter rather than black. With
 *  EdgeColour black this is exactly SimSignedRamp from the sim's debug shader.
 *
 *  Scale is the debug view's DebugScale inverted -- larger means more contrast,
 *  more saturated band cores, and a narrower dark line between them.
 *
 *  Band comes in signed and unclamped, so this is where the range is decided.
 *  Clamping earlier would have thrown it away before the ramp could use it. */
float3 GG_BandColour(float Band, float3 ColourA, float3 ColourB, float3 EdgeColour, float Scale)
{
    const float T = clamp(Band * Scale, -1.0f, 1.0f);

    const float3 Hue = (T < 0.0f) ? ColourB : ColourA;

    return lerp(EdgeColour, Hue, abs(T));
}

/** An authored vortex, added AFTER the shear rather than baked into the field
 *  that gets sheared.
 *
 *  This is not a convenience. Kinematic shear has no mechanism to sustain a
 *  vortex, so anything living inside the sheared field is eventually stretched
 *  into a filament and lost. Real spots survive because the inverse cascade
 *  feeds them continuously, and nothing here reproduces that. Exempting them
 *  from the shear is the honest fake, and it gives the right look besides: the
 *  spot persists while everything around it filaments past.
 *
 *  Centre is a unit direction. Drift its longitude by rotating Centre with
 *  GG_RotateZ at the spot's own band rate before calling. */
float GG_Spot(float3 N, float3 Centre, float Radius, float Strength)
{
    const float D = distance(N, normalize(Centre));

    return Strength * exp(-(D * D) / max(Radius * Radius, 1e-6f));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Density
//
// COVERAGE IS 100% AND THAT SIMPLIFIES EVERYTHING.
//
// Terrestrial cloud shaders spend most of their complexity on coverage: where
// is there cloud and where is there sky. A gas giant has no holes. There is no
// threshold here, no coverage remap, no cloud/no-cloud decision. Density is
// solid below the deck and falls off above it, and every visible feature is
// RELIEF in that top surface rather than variation in opacity.
//
// WHICH IS WHY BAND DRIVES ALTITUDE, NOT OPACITY.
//
// Zones and belts are not bright and dark patches of the same material. They
// are high and low cloud decks: a zone is a raised deck of fresh ammonia ice,
// a belt is where the deck sits low and the line of sight reaches warmer,
// darker material underneath. Brightness is the consequence. Feeding Band into
// density would be modelling the symptom and would look flat under any lighting
// model, because there would be no actual geometry for light to interact with.
//
// The useful side effect is that all the detail work -- ridges, mottling,
// wisps, and the edge weighting that concentrates them on transitions -- lands
// in the height field. Under a march it becomes surface relief along the cliffs
// between decks, which is where a real gas giant's texture lives.
//
// THE BAND IS NOT A DENSITY TERM EITHER.
//
// It says what the material IS, not how much of it there is, so it belongs in
// extinction and scattering colour -- which is exactly where the three
// scattering parameter sets will read it from. It is passed straight through
// below rather than composited. Folding it into density would make band
// families physically thinner, which is wrong and, worse, would look almost
// right.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------



/** Density at a point in the shell.
 *
 *  LocalPos here carries its true radius, unlike everywhere else in this file.
 *  The columnar field is evaluated at the normalized direction: the shell is
 *  thin enough that sampling at the true radius would shift UVW by far less
 *  than one lattice cell, so it would cost the same and change nothing.
 *
 *  Profile is float4(ShellThickness, DensityRamp, VortexThreshold, CoreDensity).
 *
 *  StepHalfLength is half the march step, expressed in SHELL FRACTIONS along
 *  the radius -- the amount of altitude this sample is responsible for. The
 *  density ramp is integrated across it so a step straddling the cloud top is
 *  weighted by how much of it lies inside, rather than counted or discarded
 *  whole.
 *
 *  Returns:
 *    x  Density in [0,1]. Multiply by an extinction coefficient in the march.
 *    y  BAND, signed. For GG_BandColour. NOT a density term.
 *    z  Pressure, for tinting vortices.
 *    w  The deck top at this column, as a shell fraction. The march uses it to
 *       skip the empty space above the deck instead of stepping through it.
 *
 *  BandSharpness multiplies the already-normalized vorticity, so 1 is neutral
 *  and the useful range is roughly 0.5 to 3. Too high flattens the elevation
 *  toward its asymptote everywhere but the band boundaries, which turns the
 *  height field into terraces joined by cliffs and throws away the relief this
 *  exists to produce. */
float4 GG_Density(
	Texture2DArray FlowField,
	SamplerState FlowSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 LocalPos,
	float PlanetRadius,
	float Time,
	float RigidRate,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float3 Layers,
	float BandSharpness,
	float ReliefThinning,
	float DetailVertical,
	float DetailErosion,
	float DetailRelief,
	float DetailDepth,
	float StepHalfLength,
	float DensityCurve,
	float4 Relief,
	float4 Profile)
{
    const float Radius = length(LocalPos);
    const float3 Direction = LocalPos / max(Radius, 1e-4f);

	// Shell fraction. 0 at the deck base, 1 at the top of the modelled shell.
    const float Height = (Radius - PlanetRadius) / max(Profile.x, 1e-4f);

    const float4 Field = GG_Field(
		FlowField, FlowSampler, PackedDetail, PackedSampler,
		Direction, Time, RigidRate, Scales, Warps, Detail, Layers,
		Height, BandSharpness, ReliefThinning, DetailVertical, DetailRelief, Relief, Profile.z);

    const float Top = Field.x;

	// THE RAMP IS INTEGRATED ACROSS THE STEP, NOT SAMPLED AT ITS MIDPOINT.
	//
	// One midpoint sample makes the deck a near-binary decision per step: a
	// step whose centre lands above Top contributes nothing and one just below
	// contributes whatever the ramp has reached. As the surface drifts against
	// the step lattice that flips, and the flip locus is a line on screen --
	// an artifact of the sampling rather than of the field.
	//
	// The deck top varies slowly across one step, so Top is evaluated once at
	// the centre and only the RAMP is integrated. Three evaluations of a
	// closed-form curve, no extra fetches, which is why this is worth doing
	// here rather than by taking more steps.
    const float HalfStep = max(StepHalfLength, 0.0f);

    const float Ramp = max(Profile.y, 1e-4f);
    const float Core = max(Profile.w, 0.0f);

    const float RampNear = GG_DensityRamp(Top - (Height - HalfStep), Ramp, DensityCurve, Core);
    const float RampMid = GG_DensityRamp(Top - Height, Ramp, DensityCurve, Core);
    const float RampFar = GG_DensityRamp(Top - (Height + HalfStep), Ramp, DensityCurve, Core);

	// Simpson across the step.
    const float Gradient = (RampNear + 4.0f * RampMid + RampFar) / 6.0f;

	// Depth at the sample centre, for the carve falloff only.
    const float Depth = Top - Height;

	// Full erosion at the surface, none below DetailDepth. Without this the
	// noise carves equally at every depth, so the core never overcomes it and
	// the deck is uniformly pitted rather than being solid material with a
	// worked surface.
	//
	// Smoothstep rather than a linear ramp: the point where erosion stops is a
	// density discontinuity in the depth direction, and a linear falloff leaves
	// it C0. That shows up edge-on as a faint shelf at a constant altitude.
    const float CarveWeight = 1.0f - smoothstep(0.0f, max(DetailDepth, 1e-4f), Depth);

	// THE CARVE MODULATES THE GRADIENT AND NOTHING ELSE.
	//
	// Multiplicative, and the gradient is exactly zero above Top, so nothing
	// the noise does can put density outside the bound GG_TopBounds computed --
	// which is what keeps the marched span analytically thin.
	//
	// The lerp floors the carve at (1 - DetailErosion) rather than reaching
	// zero. There is no lower cloud shell, so a fully transparent column would
	// let a ray run to the far side of the planet; the lerp cannot produce one
	// however dark the noise gets, as long as DetailErosion stays below 1.
    const float Density = Gradient * lerp(1.0f, Field.w, saturate(DetailErosion) * CarveWeight);

	// W IS THE DECK TOP AT THIS COLUMN.
	//
	// The march needs it for two things. It integrates the sample's own
	// altitude for the surface report -- and it can compute that itself from
	// the sample position, so passing it back would waste the channel. What it
	// cannot compute is where the deck IS, and it needs that to skip the empty
	// space above it rather than stepping through it.
	//
	// OLD NOTE, kept because the reasoning still applies to the altitude the
	// march integrates: Top is constant down a column, so integrating Top along
	// the ray returns Top exactly whatever the density did on the way. The
	// SAMPLE HEIGHT is what varies, and weighting it by density gives the mean
	// altitude the ray actually saw.
	//
	// This is what makes the 3D detail visible at all, and the distinction is
	// easy to miss. Top is constant down a column: it comes from the flow, and
	// the flow is columnar. So integrating Top along the ray returns Top
	// exactly, whatever the density did on the way -- and the detail, which
	// only affects density, could never reach the image through it.
	//
	// Integrating the SAMPLE HEIGHT weighted by density gives the mean altitude
	// of what the ray actually saw. Where the noise has eroded the deck the ray
	// penetrates further before saturating, so that mean drops. The 3D
	// structure becomes surface relief without ever displacing a surface, which
	// is the only way a heightfield and a volume can both be honoured: a
	// displacement needs one value per column, and a volume does not have one.
	//
	// Field.z is the signed band, which is what the colour follows.
    return float4(Density, Field.z, Field.y, Top);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Raymarch
//
// A SHELL, NOT A BOX.
//
// NoiseVolumePreview marches a box, and the note at the top of that file
// explains why: a tiling volume's domain is a cube and the seams it exists to
// find sit on the faces. Neither reason survives here. This field is not being
// validated for tiling, it is a spherical atmosphere, and the same file records
// that the galaxy marcher it descended from used a sphere for exactly this
// reason. So this goes back to a sphere, and specifically to a thin SHELL.
//
// THE SPAN IS ANALYTICALLY THIN, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS AFFORDABLE.
//
// Each density sample here costs three texture fetches, against one for the
// box marcher. Stepping the whole atmosphere at that price would be hopeless.
//
// It is not necessary. GG_CloudTop is a bounded expression: Band and Pressure
// are both bounded, and the storm term is one-sided, so the highest and lowest
// the deck can possibly reach are known in closed form from Relief alone. Above
// TopMax there is provably nothing, so the cull sphere sits exactly there.
//
// There is no inner bound: the deck is solid all the way down and the ray ends
// on transmittance. What keeps that affordable is the empty-space skip, which
// jumps a ray straight to the deck rather than stepping through the vacuum a
// grazing ray would otherwise cross. This is the
// same argument the baker's brick bounds make -- bound the field, then use the
// bound to avoid sampling it -- applied to an analytic term rather than a baked
// one, which makes it exact rather than conservative.
//
// The consequence is that step budget buys resolution of the CLOUD TOP rather
// than of the atmosphere, which is the only thing visible at 100% coverage.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Per-pixel seed for the march, from the integer pixel coordinate.
 *
 *  A proper integer hash rather than the frac-of-a-large-multiple trick that
 *  was here before. That one is cheap and adequate for a single offset, but it
 *  correlates badly under the repeated advance below -- neighbouring pixels
 *  take similar walks through the sequence, which is exactly the case that
 *  needs them not to.
 *
 *  Lifted from the atmosphere raymarcher so both use the same sequence. */
float GG_RaySeed(float2 PixelPos)
{
    const uint2 P = (uint2) PixelPos;

    uint Seed = P.x * 1664525u + P.y * 1013904223u;
    Seed = (Seed ^ (Seed >> 16)) * 2654435769u;
    Seed = (Seed ^ (Seed >> 16));

    return (float) Seed / 4294967295.0f;
}

/** Offset for one step, in [-0.5, 0.5] before scaling.
 *
 *  EVERY STEP IS JITTERED, NOT JUST THE FIRST. This is the whole reason the
 *  deck was slicing.
 *
 *  Offsetting only the first step translates the sampling lattice per pixel,
 *  which decorrelates neighbouring rays but leaves each ray on a perfectly
 *  regular grid. A regular grid beats against any structure with a comparable
 *  period, and the density ramp has exactly that -- so the deck came out in
 *  layers at the step spacing, stable in world space and therefore reading as
 *  geometry rather than as noise.
 *
 *  Advancing the seed by the golden ratio each step turns the ray's samples
 *  into a low-discrepancy sequence instead: still stratified, one sample per
 *  step, but with no period for the field to resonate with. The 0.618033
 *  advance is the standard choice because its continued fraction converges
 *  slowest, which is the formal statement of having no short period. */
float GG_StepJitter(float Seed, int StepIndex)
{
    return frac(Seed + (float) StepIndex * 0.618033f) - 0.5f;
}


/** Ray against a sphere centred on the origin. Direction must be normalized.
 *
 *  Returns false when the ray misses. T0 may be negative, which means the
 *  origin is inside; callers clamp rather than reject, since a camera inside
 *  the shell is a normal case and not an error. */
bool GG_RaySphere(float3 Origin, float3 Dir, float Radius, out float T0, out float T1)
{
    const float B = dot(Origin, Dir);
    const float C = dot(Origin, Origin) - Radius * Radius;

    const float Disc = B * B - C;

    if (Disc < 0.0f)
    {
        T0 = 0.0f;
        T1 = 0.0f;
        return false;
    }

    const float S = sqrt(Disc);

    T0 = -B - S;
    T1 = -B + S;

    return true;
}

/** A conservative UPPER BOUND on the cloud top at one direction, from a single
 *  flow fetch.
 *
 *  Used to find where a ray enters the deck, which is a different job from
 *  integrating it and wants a different tool.
 *
 *  WHY AN UPPER BOUND AND NOT THE VALUE. A search that overestimates the deck
 *  stops short and takes another iteration; one that underestimates steps over
 *  the surface and misses cloud entirely. Only one of those is recoverable, so
 *  every term here is taken at its most generous:
 *
 *    - ReliefScale is in [0,1] and multiplies a term that changes sign, so the
 *      band contribution is max(Relief.y * (Elevation - 0.5), 0).
 *    - The storm tower is taken at full height wherever its gate is open,
 *      rather than fetching the cell mask that would thin it.
 *    - The detail carve is omitted, which is safe precisely because it only
 *      ever LOWERS the deck.
 *
 *  ONE FETCH, not three. The deck is dominated by the flow; the two baked
 *  volume reads only refine it, and refinement is exactly what a conservative
 *  search does not need. That makes the search phase cheaper per iteration than
 *  the march it replaces. */
float GG_CloudTopUpper(
	Texture2DArray FlowField,
	SamplerState FlowSampler,
	float3 P,
	float Time,
	float RigidRate,
	float FlowLayer,
	float BandBias,
	float BandSharpness,
	float4 Relief,
	float VortexThreshold)
{
    const float3 N = normalize(GG_RotateZ(P, -RigidRate * Time));

    const float4 Flow = GG_SampleFlow(FlowField, FlowSampler, N, FlowLayer);

    const float Zeta = (Flow.w + BandBias) * BandSharpness;
    const float Elevation = 1.0f - exp(-Zeta * Zeta);
    const float Pressure = Flow.z;

    float Top = Relief.x + max(Relief.y * (Elevation - 0.5f), 0.0f);

    Top += Relief.z * Pressure;

    Top += max(Relief.w, 0.0f) * smoothstep(VortexThreshold, 1.0f, abs(Pressure));

    return Top;
}

/** Closed-form bounds on the cloud top, used to make the marched span thin.
 *
 *  EVERY TERM IN GG_CloudTop HAS TO APPEAR HERE. An underestimate is not a
 *  conservative error, it is a clipping one: the outer radius comes from
 *  TopMax, so a term left out slices the tops off exactly the features it
 *  belongs to, and the result reads as a geometry bug rather than a bounds one.
 *
 *  Elevation is in [0,1], so the band contributes half of Relief.y either side
 *  of the base. Pressure is soft-saturated to [-1,1] sim-side, so it
 *  contributes the whole of Relief.z. ReliefScale never exceeds one, so
 *  ignoring it is conservative in the safe direction. */
void GG_TopBounds(float4 Relief, float DetailRelief, out float OutMin, out float OutMax)
{
    const float Span = 0.5f * abs(Relief.y) + abs(Relief.z);

	// The detail carve is subtractive: Shape is in [0,1] and enters as
	// (1 - Shape), so it lowers the deck by at most DetailRelief and never
	// raises it. It therefore belongs in the LOWER bound only.
	//
	// That is worth having deliberately rather than by accident. A symmetric
	// term would push TopMax up, and TopMax sets the cull radius -- so it would
	// lengthen the chord every grazing ray has to cross, for lumps that only
	// ever cut downward.
    OutMin = Relief.x - Span - abs(DetailRelief);
    OutMax = Relief.x + Span + max(Relief.w, 0.0f);
}



/** Marches the cloud shell and integrates density.
 *
 *  InViewVec       Local camera vector, surface toward camera. CameraVector
 *                  through TransformVector [World -> Local].
 *  InCamPosLocal   Camera position in PLANET-LOCAL space, in the same units as
 *                  PlanetRadius. This is the ray origin, so a wrong value moves
 *                  the ray rather than merely biasing step size.
 *
 *  DensityCurve    Onset sharpness. Higher reaches opacity faster; 1 is smooth
 *                  and 2 or above creases along the deck. See GG_DensityRamp.
 *
 *  InStepBudget    A budget, not a count. See the note in NoiseVolumePreview.
 *  InStepGrowth    Step growth across the marched span.
 *  InMaxSteps      Safety bound. Set to int(StepBudget) + 8.
 *  InPixelPos      Parameters.SvPosition.xy at the Custom node.
 *  InJitterAmount  Scales the per-step offset. 1 is correct and not a
 *                  compromise: the offset stays inside the step it represents,
 *                  so the estimator is unbiased. Reducing it puts the sampling
 *                  lattice back, which is what produces slicing.
 *
 *  Returns float4:
 *    x  Cloud top altitude at the visible surface. Use it for shading relief
 *       and for sphere-tracing the surface. Note what is NOT returned:
 *       accumulated emission. For a uniformly emitting medium the emission
 *       integral is identically 1 - Transmittance, so it carries no structure
 *       and is recoverable from y anyway.
 *    y  Transmittance. 1 - this is opacity.
 *    z  BAND, signed and unclamped. Feed to GG_BandColour. This is the same
 *       quantity the debug view draws as blue against orange.
 *    w  Pressure, for tinting vortices. Negative is cyclonic.
 *
 *  x and z are deliberately different quantities. x is the MAGNITUDE of the
 *  vorticity, turned into height: low at boundaries, high in band interiors.
 *  z is its SIGN, turned into a colour choice. The debug view shows both at
 *  once as one image, which makes them easy to mistake for one channel.
 *
 *  z and w are integrated against the same weights as the density itself, so
 *  each is the expected value over what the ray actually sees rather than a
 *  reading from an arbitrary step. That is what makes them usable as shading
 *  inputs: at the limb, where a ray grazes many columns, a single-step reading
 *  would flicker.
 *
 *  Packed into one float4 rather than returned through out parameters because
 *  the Custom node's Additional Outputs have to be declared by hand in the
 *  node's own array, and a mismatch there fails at shader compile with an
 *  undeclared identifier rather than anywhere useful. Four channels is what a
 *  single return can carry, and these are the four that matter. */
float4 GG_March(
	Texture2DArray FlowField,
	SamplerState FlowSampler,
	Texture3D PackedDetail,
	SamplerState PackedSampler,
	float3 InViewVec,
	float3 InCamPosLocal,
	float PlanetRadius,
	float Time,
	float RigidRate,
	float4 Scales,
	float4 Warps,
	float4 Detail,
	float3 Layers,
	float BandSharpness,
	float ReliefThinning,
	float DetailVertical,
	float DetailErosion,
	float DetailRelief,
	float DetailDepth,
	float DensityCurve,
	float4 Relief,
	float4 Profile,
	float InStepBudget,
	float InStepGrowth,
	int InMaxSteps,
	float2 InPixelPos,
	float InJitterAmount)
{
	// Miss value. Transmittance 1 means nothing was hit, which is what every
	// early exit below wants to report.
    const float4 Miss = float4(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);

    const float3 Origin = InCamPosLocal;
    const float3 Dir = normalize(-InViewVec);

    const float Thickness = max(Profile.x, 1e-4f);

	// -- Shell bounds -------------------------------------------------------

    float TopMin, TopMax;
    GG_TopBounds(Relief, DetailRelief, TopMin, TopMax);

	// THE ONLY SPHERE, AND IT IS A CULL, NOT A BOUNDARY.
	//
	// Density is exactly zero above Top, and Top can never exceed TopMax, so
	// everything outside this radius is provably empty. The sphere exists to
	// avoid marching that emptiness and for no other reason -- nothing is
	// clipped by it and nothing terminates on it.
	//
	// NO MARGIN. Density is exactly zero above Top and Top can never exceed
	// TopMax, so anything outside this radius is provably empty and padding it
	// only lengthens the chord a grazing ray has to cross.
	//
	// A margin was added earlier to give the ray a run-up rather than landing
	// its first sample on the density boundary. That is no longer a concern:
	// the ramp is integrated across each step, so a step straddling the surface
	// is weighted by how much of it lies inside rather than sampled at a point.
    const float OuterRadius = PlanetRadius + Thickness * TopMax;

	// Used only for step sizing below, where the ramp's own depth is the scale
	// the deck has to be resolved against.
    const float RampDepth = 1.0f / max(Profile.y, 1e-4f);

	// THERE IS NO INNER BOUND. THE DECK HAS A TOP AND NOTHING ELSE.
	//
	// A terrestrial cloud shader has a band with an inner and an outer radius,
	// and clipping the march against both is right there. This deck is not a
	// band: it is solid from its top all the way down, getting denser, and the
	// only thing that ends a ray is running out of transmittance.
	//
	// The inner sphere was carried over from that shader and every problem it
	// caused was the same problem in a different form. Forcing opacity on
	// contact made its silhouette a hard edge. Clipping the span without
	// forcing opacity made the STEP SIZE jump at the same silhouette, because
	// the step was span-relative. Both drew a line along a sphere that has no
	// business existing here.
	//
	// Rays heading inward need no help terminating. Height falls to
	// -PlanetRadius/Thickness at the centre -- tens of shell depths -- so
	// the ramp saturates long before and a single step goes opaque. The early-out
	// on transmittance handles it, which is what it is for.

    float TEnter, TExit;
    if (!GG_RaySphere(Origin, Dir, OuterRadius, TEnter, TExit))
    {
        return Miss;
    }

    if (TExit <= 0.0f)
    {
        return Miss;
    }

	// Camera inside the shell falls out of this clamp rather than needing its
	// own branch.
    float TStart = max(TEnter, 0.0f);

    if (TExit <= TStart)
    {
        return Miss;
    }

	// -- Step sizing --------------------------------------------------------

    const float Span = max(TExit - TStart, 1e-6f);
	// STEP SIZE FROM A FIXED REFERENCE, NOT FROM THIS RAY'S SPAN.
	//
	// It used to be Span / StepBudget, and the span is clipped by the inner
	// bound -- so it changes discontinuously at that sphere's tangent, and the
	// step size changed with it. A step-size jump in an integral this sparse
	// does not average out: it shows as a clean line along the silhouette of a
	// sphere that is supposed to be invisible.
	//
	// The reference is the deck's own total vertical extent, identical for
	// every ray. StepBudget therefore means steps across the DECK rather than
	// steps across whatever this ray happened to be clipped to, which is also
	// the more useful reading -- the deck is the thing being resolved, and how
	// much empty shell a ray crossed first should not change how finely it is
	// sampled.
    const float ReferenceSpan = max(Thickness * (TopMax - TopMin + RampDepth), 1e-6f);

    const float BaseStep = ReferenceSpan / max(InStepBudget, 1.0f);

	// NO STEP FLOOR.
	//
	// There used to be one, to stop the march resolving finer than the field's
	// own finest feature. It was a guard against a span-relative BaseStep going
	// arbitrarily small on a short span -- and BaseStep is now derived from a
	// fixed reference, so it cannot.
	//
	// What the floor did instead was introduce a max(), and a max() is a C1
	// discontinuity wherever its two arguments cross. Because the crossing
	// happens at a fixed distance along the ray, and every ray starts at the
	// camera, that locus is a SPHERE AROUND THE VIEWER -- which draws a curve
	// across the image that moves with the camera and belongs to nothing in the
	// field. With BaseStep and the old floor landing within a few percent of
	// each other at typical settings, the crossing sat near the ray start,
	// where the weights are largest and it shows most.
	//
	// StepBudget is the only control on step size now, which is the honest
	// arrangement: one number, no branch, no locus.

    const float Growth = max(InStepGrowth, 0.0f);
    const float InvSpan = 1.0f / ReferenceSpan;

    const float RaySeed = GG_RaySeed(InPixelPos);
    const float JitterAmount = saturate(InJitterAmount);

	// -- March --------------------------------------------------------------

    float Transmittance = 1.0f;
    float Emission = 0.0f;
    float BandAccum = 0.0f;
    float Pressure = 0.0f;

	// SURFACE ALTITUDE AS AN OPACITY-WEIGHTED MEAN.
	//
	// Every ray that accumulates anything has one, and it varies continuously
	// with everything that feeds it. That is the property that matters here.
	//
	// It was briefly a transmittance CROSSING instead -- interpolate where
	// opacity passes a half -- which is sharper for rays that reach it and
	// undefined for rays that do not. The fallback for those was the exit
	// altitude on the far side of the cull sphere, unrelated to the deck, so
	// rays that just barely saturated reported a height inside the cloud and
	// rays that just barely did not reported somewhere else entirely. The
	// boundary between the two populations is the set of rays whose total
	// optical depth is ln(2), and that locus traces the deck's silhouette --
	// drawing a line along the surface it was measuring.
	//
	// The crossing existed to fix per-pixel noise in this value, which came
	// from rays saturating inside a single step. That had a different cause and
	// has its own fix: the step floor is gone and BaseStep is derived from a
	// fixed reference, so several samples now land inside the skin and the mean
	// is stable without needing to be sharp.
    float TopAccum = 0.0f;

	// -- Search: find where the ray enters the deck -------------------------
	//
	// SEPARATE FROM THE MARCH, AND DETERMINISTIC, WHICH IS THE POINT.
	//
	// This used to be a skip inside the accumulation loop, and mixing the two
	// jobs is what made every fix produce a new artifact. A search wants to be
	// deterministic and convergent; an integrator wants to be jittered and
	// unbiased. Sharing one loop means every guard added for one becomes a
	// visible locus for the other -- a HARD edge where a deterministic
	// threshold fires, a DITHERED edge where a jittered step decides whether a
	// ray accumulates at all. Both were visible at once, on opposite sides of
	// the same band, which is what gave the split away.
	//
	// Separated, each does its own job. The search crosses a region that is
	// provably empty, so it contributes no light and needs no jitter; the march
	// starts from a smoothly varying entry point and carries the only jitter in
	// the system.
	//
	// Sphere-traced against the conservative upper bound, ONE fetch per
	// iteration rather than three. The factor is below one because that bound
	// is vertical only and the deck also varies HORIZONTALLY -- a ray descending
	// toward a low column may pass over a taller one on the way.
    float TSearch = TStart;

	[loop]
    for (int Probe = 0; Probe < 24; Probe++)
    {
        const float3 ProbePos = Origin + Dir * TSearch;
        const float ProbeRadius = length(ProbePos);
        const float ProbeHeight = (ProbeRadius - PlanetRadius) / Thickness;

        const float3 ProbeDir = ProbePos / max(ProbeRadius, 1e-4f);
        const float ProbeRate = dot(Dir, ProbeDir);

        const float TopUpper = GG_CloudTopUpper(
			FlowField, FlowSampler, ProbeDir, Time, RigidRate,
			Layers.x, Warps.z, BandSharpness, Relief, Profile.z);

		// Inside the deck. The march takes over from here.
        if (ProbeHeight <= TopUpper)
        {
            break;
        }

		// Climbing AND clear of the highest the deck can ever reach. Both
		// conditions matter: a climbing ray still inside the deck's range can
		// meet a taller column ahead, so ProbeRate alone is not enough to say
		// nothing is left.
        if (ProbeRate >= 0.0f && ProbeHeight > TopMax)
        {
            return Miss;
        }

		// THE DECK RISES TO MEET THE RAY, AND THAT HAS TO BE IN THE DENOMINATOR.
		//
		// Dividing the gap by the descent rate alone assumes the ray closes it
		// by DESCENDING. Near tangent it barely descends, so that advance goes
		// to infinity -- and yet the ray still reaches the deck, because the
		// deck climbs toward it. The trace overshot by orders of magnitude on
		// exactly those rays, landing at an entry point that jumped around with
		// view angle, which drew slices along tangent-aligned directions.
		//
		// A ray closes the gap at the sum of two rates: its own descent, and
		// the deck's rise across the ground it covers. With the deck's slope
		// bounded by DeckSlope in shell fractions per radian, the horizontal
		// half converts through Thickness / Radius -- shell per radian into
		// local per local, matching the descent rate's units.
		//
		// At tangent the descent term vanishes and the slope term carries it,
		// which is what makes the advance finite there instead of unbounded.
		// This is a cone trace rather than a sphere trace, and the cone angle
		// is what the deck's own roughness allows.
        const float Horizontal = sqrt(max(1.0f - ProbeRate * ProbeRate, 0.0f));

        const float ClosingRate = -ProbeRate
			+ Horizontal * max(Layers.z, 0.1f) * Thickness / max(ProbeRadius, 1e-4f);

        const float Advance = (ProbeHeight - TopUpper) * Thickness / max(ClosingRate, 1e-5f);

		// 0.9 rather than a full step: the bound is conservative but DeckSlope
		// is authored, and an under-declared slope is the one way this can
		// still step over the surface.
        TSearch += max(Advance * 0.9f, 1e-4f);

        if (TSearch >= TExit)
        {
            return Miss;
        }
    }

    float T = TSearch;
    float StepsTaken = 0.0f;

	[loop]
    for (int i = 0; i < InMaxSteps; i++)
    {
        const float Remaining = TExit - T;
        if (Remaining <= 1e-6f)
        {
            break;
        }

		// Growth runs on distance travelled against the same fixed reference,
		// so it carries no span dependence either.
        const float T01 = saturate((T - TStart) * InvSpan);

        float StepLen = BaseStep * (1.0f + Growth * T01);
        StepLen = min(StepLen, Remaining);

        StepsTaken += 1.0f;

		// Sampled at the step centre plus a jittered offset within the step, so
		// the sample stays inside the interval it represents -- stratified,
		// unbiased, and off the lattice.
        const float Offset = GG_StepJitter(RaySeed, i) * StepLen * JitterAmount;

        const float3 SamplePos = Origin + Dir * (T + StepLen * 0.5f + Offset);

		// How much ALTITUDE this step spans, which is the step length projected
		// onto the radius. A grazing ray covers almost none of it however long
		// the step is, so using the step length directly would smear the deck
		// across the whole limb.
        const float3 SampleDir = SamplePos * rsqrt(max(dot(SamplePos, SamplePos), 1e-8f));

		// How fast altitude changes per unit of ray length. Near zero for a
		// grazing ray, which is exactly the case the skip below has to handle.
		// Magnitude only. The sign mattered when the skip lived in this loop;
		// the search owns that now, and all this feeds is the step's altitude
		// extent, which is symmetric.
        const float RadialRate = abs(dot(Dir, SampleDir));

        const float SampleHeight = (length(SamplePos) - PlanetRadius) / Thickness;

        const float StepHalfHeight = RadialRate * StepLen * 0.5f / max(Profile.x, 1e-4f);

        const float4 Field = GG_Density(
			FlowField, FlowSampler, PackedDetail, PackedSampler,
			SamplePos, PlanetRadius,
			Time, RigidRate, Scales, Warps, Detail, Layers,
			BandSharpness, ReliefThinning, DetailVertical, DetailErosion, DetailRelief,
			DetailDepth, StepHalfHeight, DensityCurve, Relief, Profile);

		// No extinction multiplier. Density is already absolute -- CoreDensity is
		// the density the deck reaches, in world units -- so a separate scale
		// would only be a second way to say the same thing, and the two would
		// have to be kept in step by hand.
        const float Alpha = 1.0f - exp(-Field.x * StepLen);
        const float Weight = Alpha * Transmittance;

        Emission += Weight;
        BandAccum += Weight * Field.y;
        Pressure += Weight * Field.z;

        TopAccum += Weight * SampleHeight;

        Transmittance *= 1.0f - Alpha;

        if (Transmittance < 0.001f)
        {
            break;
        }

		// EMPTY SPACE IS SKIPPED, NOT STEPPED THROUGH.
		//
		// The step size is chosen to resolve the DECK, which is a fraction of a
		// unit thick. A ray at the limb enters the cull sphere far above the
		// deck and travels a chord tens of units long before reaching it --
		// hundreds of steps at the deck's own resolution, against a step budget
		// sized for the deck.
		//
		// So grazing rays exhausted MaxSteps in vacuum and exited transparent,
		// while rays a fraction of a degree steeper reached density and went
		// opaque. The threshold between the two is geometric and sharp, and it
		// drew a hard silhouette exactly where the limb should be softest.
		//
		// Above the deck the density is provably zero, so the ray can jump
		// straight for it. Halved because the deck top varies horizontally and
		// a neighbouring column may be higher: an underestimate costs a step, an
		// overestimate steps over the surface.
		//
		// Guarded on RadialRate rather than trusting the divide -- a ray running
		// exactly tangent changes altitude at no rate at all, and the distance
		// to a deck it never descends toward is not finite.
		// NO SKIP HERE, AND NO DIRECTION GUARD EITHER.
		//
		// The search above already placed the ray at the deck. A ray that
		// climbs back out mid-march is rare enough that stepping the gap costs
		// less than another threshold would -- and every threshold in this loop
		// is a locus, which is what the last several rounds of artifacts all
		// turned out to be.

        T += StepLen;
    }

	// Normalize the integrated properties by the weight that produced them, so
	// they stay in their own units instead of scaling with opacity. Guarded
	// because a ray that grazed the shell without accumulating anything has
	// nothing to report and must not divide by zero.

    const float TotalWeight = max(Emission, 1e-4f);

	// Normalized by the weight that produced it, so it stays an altitude rather
	// than scaling with opacity. A ray that gathered nothing divides by the
	// guard and reports zero, which is correct: it saw no surface.
    float TopOut = TopAccum / TotalWeight;

#if GG_DEBUG_STEPS
	TopOut = StepsTaken / max((float)InMaxSteps, 1.0f);
#endif

    return float4(
		TopOut,
		Transmittance,
		BandAccum / TotalWeight,
		Pressure / TotalWeight);
}
